hi I'm Nick Gillespie with reason TV and  today we're talking with Chris Edwards  of the Cato Institute he's the director  of Tax Policy Studies there and chris  has just released the fiscal policy  report card on America's governors for  2010 Chris thanks for coming who are the  best governors and why well every two  years we score the nation's 50 governors  and we give them sort of school grades a  for the good guys down to F for the  really bad governors and this year we  gave a's to four governors joe Manchin  West Virginia Democrat mark Sanford of  South Carolina Republican tim Pawlenty  Republican of Minnesota and bobby Jindal  Republican of Louisiana okay let's talk  about Sanford first is what was so good  about him besides the fact he was too  busy getting his ass kicked by the press  and his wife to really do much in terms  of increasing legislation in taxes well  mark Sanford really has been a champion  of both limited taxation and limited  spending in South Carolina remarkably  he's been in office for eight years and  the budget this year in South Carolina  is basically exactly the same dollar  amount as it was back in 2003 so he's  limited spending by vetoing dozens and  dozens of expensive spending bills and  on the tax front he's pushed really hard  for income tax cuts and sales tax cuts  what about somebody like mansion in West  Virginia who's now going into the US  Senate well Joe Manchin is very  interesting and he said he's a Democrat  he's been in office also eight years  he's been a very frugal on the on the  spending side he's pushed to cut  spending many years he's been in office  but really his biggest a favorable  attribute it is his business tax cuts he  has eliminated the franchise tax in West  Virginia he's eliminated license fees on  businesses and he's cut the corporate  income tax in West Virginia so he really  understands that his state's got to be  competitive in the national and world  economy so what what makes for an  effective tax cut you've talked about  how they get phased in over time how  does that work and what makes for an  effective spending cut is it all done it  once or is that phased in over time well  state governments work substantially  differently than if  the federal government can run deficits  and lessly it seems so if you cut taxes  at the federal level it doesn't mean the  politicians have to cut spending because  they can run big deficits at the state  level virtually all the states they have  to balance their budgets every year  either by tradition or statute or their  constitutions so when you want to cut  taxes at the state level you will often  phase them in overtime so the revenue  loss is a little bit at a time and you  can shift down spending too so that you  keep the budget balanced on the spending  side you know there's a there's really  no nothing prevents a governor's from  cutting spending whenever they want and  of course the recent years there's been  lots of efforts in places like Colorado  and South Carolina to put camps on the  overall budget so that it doesn't grow  faster than inflation are those  effective generally or are they mostly  symbolic there's a lot of states have  these sort of budget constraints but  they don't work very well in most states  Colorado is famous tabor has it's been  the most effective you know the idea is  that you don't want the government  growing spending during boom times  faster than the population is growing  because of course as we see in places  like Colorado during boom years they  spend too much and then the bust comes  and then the government runs giant  deficits right and you actually you talk  about how between two thousand and two  thousand eight state and local spending  rose fifty five percent on average Oh  what did that go into do we have  anything to show for that at the state  level well really it expanded government  you know all over the place Medicaid of  course hasn't been growing explosively  at the state level a state local level  of Prisons really all kinds of different  spending in education of course what  about a like in you said in South  Carolina it's basically the same as it  was in 2003 the total sprite re South  Carolinians doing okay with that or are  they wearing you know pickle barrel's  for clothes and walking on their feet or  what I think what you're asking nick is  can we get by with less government of  course the answer it is absolutely less  I mean the interesting thing the last  few years is that most states have cut  their budgets at least their general  fund budgets I mean even the left Lee  Democrats in places like Maryland have  cut their budgets pretty substantially  because they're forced to because they  have to balance their budget every year  now you do talk about the amount of debt  that states are in I mean that's  exploded over the over this decade  what's going on with that if in fact  they're cutting spending well really an  interesting thing that's been happening  with state governments is you go back  and look in the 1990s total state and  local government debt United States was  essentially flat for the whole decade at  about a trillion dollars this decade  state and local debt has exploded it's  more than doubled its over 2 trillion  dollars now so more and more states they  used to only go to the debt markets to  fund their capital investments more and  more they're using debt to fund their  regular operating expenses you have said  that you had a couple of surprises in  this somebody like Tim Pawlenty who's  often brooded about as a leading  Republican presidential candidate in  2012 what was he doing in Minnesota and  why did that surprise you well Tim  Pawlenty I you know honest I didn't know  much about him before I wrote up this  report I knew he was a social  conservative but I didn't know whether  it was a fiscal conservative he's been  fantastic he's been in office for eight  years he's been a frugal on the spending  side and on tax cuts he's been great he  has a left-leaning legislature that  keeps sending him tax hike bills he  vetoes them over and over his vetoed  income tax hikes and gas tax hikes and  alcohol gas tax hikes so he's been  really great he's cut spending during  the recession and he's absolutely  refused to raise taxes we take it for  granted in the pro free market you know  universe world that if you cut spending  if you keep spending down if you keep  taxes down everything will be okay does  this report kind of link the pro-growth  or what we presume are pro-growth  policies among governor's to actual  economic growth in the in their states  how do we know beyond satisfying our  kind of Vidya logical constraints that  this is actually doing something good  for the people who live under a temple  NT or a mark Sanford well there's no  doubt that the 50 states compete against  each other for investment for people for  high-income people and there's a lot of  variables that go into attracting  businesses and a vest  and of course you know nice climate like  Florida and Arizona of course attract a  lot of retirees the you know a lot of  the sites you mean unbearably hot for  anybody under about six well since the  invention of by air conditioning of  course six or seven decades ago that  there's been a huge magnet drawing  American side with and so you know in  addition this other states of course  generally have lower taxes in the  northeastern states and that there's a  lot more right-to-work states in the  South in other words states that don't  have forced collective bargaining and  unionization so all these factors go  into drawing business investment and so  you can look at a place like West  Virginia and governor there Joe Manchin  he's cut taxes but he's done a lot of  other pro pro friend business friendly  policies as well and the West Virginia  economy has done very well how does the  stimulus dollars that have been poured  into state budgets over the past 18  months or and I guess we're a little bit  longer now how is that affecting the way  that their financing things over the  over the time that this report covers  well I I think federal aid to the states  which you know has soared in recent  decades really starting from my Lyndon  Johnson the 1960s it's a cancer on  American government because it reduces  the responsibility for state local  governments to tax and fund their own  programs they get these subsidies from  the federal government for things like  Medicaid and it encourages them to over  expand these programs this is a really  bad trend to increasingly fun state  governments from from the federal  taxpayers if if you can give governor's  or state legislatures one bit of advice  based on your findings in the fiscal  report card the fiscal policy report  card what is it well I think that the  number one policy that I would propose  to state governors to get their  economies moving to grow out of this  recession is to eliminate their state  corporate income taxes state corporate  income taxes are really the stupidest  tax in America they distort businesses  they scare away investment both domestic  and international investment and they  hardly raise any money anyway so states  don't need them and we  half a dozen states that don't have  corporate income taxes and their  economies do pretty well I want to thank  Chris Edwards director of tax policy at  the Cato Institute and the author of the  fiscal policy report on America's  governors for 2010 Chris thanks for  coming by  you  you
 

